#heading(level: 1, numbering: none)[How this book was made] <how-this-book-was-made>

This book was made with Claude, an AI model made by Anthropic, at its editor's direction. It is free and will never be sold. This appendix says how it was made and what has and hasn't been checked, so that you can judge it.

*The text.* Claude was used to search the web for sources, to write up what they say and to write the chapters from those notes. Claude has never climbed, so the advice rests on the sources and on reasoning from them.

*The sources.* The #link(<sources>)[Sources appendix] lists about 380. About seven in ten are web pages from magazines, coaches, gyms and brands. About one in five is a study or a review, and many of the studies were read only as abstracts. The linked videos were chosen from their titles and descriptions and were not watched.

*The grades.* No source says at which grade a technique starts to matter, so every grade badge is an estimate. The grade notes cite the evidence where there is any: gym courses for named grade bands, a French coaching syllabus, setters' accounts and climbers' reports. These say when a technique is taught or set, which isn't the same as when you need it.

*Claims without a source.* Advice that cites no source is the book's own. Where it isn't plain how the book reached a conclusion, a note at the foot of the page says how. Each entry lists its sources at the foot, but about one in five of its steps and three in five of its "When to use it" lines have no citation of their own.

*The figures and the website.* No image generator was used. Claude was used to write the code that draws every figure, the cover included, and the code that builds the website. Most poses were compared with photographs or video frames, which aren't in the book.

*The checks.* Claude was also used to compare a selection of claims with the pages they cite. Each edition lists the corrections to the one before. No outside expert has reviewed this book, and no person has audited its citations.

*The records.* The repository at #link("https://github.com/simongray/ReadingTheWall")[github.com/#sym.zws;simongray/#sym.zws;ReadingTheWall] holds the research notes, the logs of the checks, the code and the list of corrections between editions (CHANGELOG.md).
